snif NEWS -- history of user-visible changes

Version 1.07
------------

* libsnif: the control-message builders (snif_conn_start, _forward,
  _reject, _abuse, _msg) compared strlen() with an int length minus a
  margin, so a buffer smaller than the margin passed the check and
  sprintf() wrote past it. They now return 0 for such a buffer.
  snif_conn_abuse() also reserves room for an 11-character score
  (-2147483648). curl_easy_setopt() gets a long for CURLOPT_VERBOSE,
  CURLOPT_UPLOAD and CURLOPT_INFILESIZE. snifd always passed large enough
  buffers, so it was not affected. No interface change; the libtool
  version is 0:1:0.

* relay: a CONNECT read from an input FIFO for a device that has no
  control connection here is held instead of dropped, and sent when the
  device connects, after this relay's own waiting clients. A held CONNECT
  is dropped on the client's CLEAR or CLOSE, or at the client timeout; at
  most SNIF_HOST_MAXHELD (4096) are held, oldest dropped first. This lets
  a client on one node of a relay cluster reach a device that connects to
  another node later, for example after an APNs wake.

* snifd: outgoing connections time out after 5 s per address
  (SNIF_SOCK_CONNTMOUT) instead of the kernel's SYN timeout, which
  stalled the whole process for about two minutes on an address that
  drops packets. This covers the connector's control and service
  connections and the relay static map.

* snifd: a -DSNIF_DEBUG build compiles again (stdio.h was missing).

* ca-proxy: a base domain can issue from a local CA (etc/<domain>.pub-ca
  and etc/<domain>.priv-ca) instead of Let's Encrypt, for closed systems
  whose connectors and relay trust a private root. snif-certd signs the
  CSR with the openssl CLI; certificates are wildcards for the device
  zone, valid 90 days, with .pub-ca as the chain. Crypt::LE,
  Crypt::OpenSSL::RSA and Net::DNS are loaded only when needed.
  snif-arc keeps such a domain's published root bundle,
  crt/<domain>.crt, instead of archiving it after 75 days.

* ca-proxy: DNS-01 in snif-certd adds its challenge values instead of
  replacing the record set, so a name and its wildcard, or two attempts
  at once, no longer remove each other's. It follows the alias of
  _acme-challenge.<host>, so several base domains can share one pool
  name, sends each update to every nameserver of the zone with a 5 s
  timeout, waits until every server that answered serves the values, and
  removes exactly its own values afterwards, retrying a failed removal
  for up to 7 days (var/snif-certd.pending).

* relay cluster (doc/cluster.md): several relay nodes can serve one set
  of names. snif-syncd keeps the CA proxy state (allocations, CSRs,
  approvals, certificates, wake tokens, counters) in step across the
  nodes over ssh; bin/snif-tunnel and snif-tunnel@.service carry each
  node's pushes to its peers. The CA proxy, snif-certd and snif-apnsd
  work through it when etc/cluster.node names the node; without it a
  relay behaves as before. See SNIF_CLUSTER_* in etc/snif.conf.

* snif-apnsd: a wake token is replaced by rename, so a reader never sees
  a partial one; its last-wake time is kept.

* snifdog: one configuration per base domain. The cron entry and
  snifdog@.service run /etc/snif/snifdog.conf and every
  /etc/snif/snifdog.d/*.conf; each base gets its own state directory,
  which snifdog refuses to share, and a lock per pass.

* ca-proxy: the VES approval limit is a leaky bucket instead of 11
  approvals per account for life. Each approval adds one and one leaks
  out per refill time; etc/<email>.ves-limit, else etc/ves-limit, sets
  the limit and the refill time (default "10 1d").

Version 1.06
------------

* PROXY protocol v1: a `+` prefix on the destination port of a connector
  port mapping (SNIF_CONN_PORTS, combines with `^`, e.g. `443:+8443` or
  `8080:^+80`) or of a relay static SNI map entry (SNIF_RELAY_MAP /
  `-m ...:+dst_port`) prepends a HAProxy PROXY protocol v1 line
  (`PROXY TCP4|TCP6 src dst sport dport`) to the forwarded stream, so the
  destination service learns the original client address instead of
  seeing every connection as coming from localhost. The destination
  listener must be configured to expect the PROXY header (nginx
  `proxy_protocol`, Apache mod_remoteip, Postfix, Dovecot, HAProxy, ...).
  On the connector the client address is as reported by the relay; on a
  relay map both addresses are taken first-hand from the client socket.

Version 1.05
------------

* relay: static SNI map. `snifd -m sni_host:fwd_port:dst_host:dst_port`
  (repeatable) forwards the still-encrypted TLS stream for a mapped SNI
  hostname arriving on the mapped relay port directly to a fixed
  destination, bypassing connector dispatch; the destination terminates
  TLS with its own certificate. Set SNIF_RELAY_MAP in etc/snif.conf to
  have bin/snif-relay pass the mappings to snifd. A mapped hostname
  arriving on a non-mapped relay port is dropped immediately instead of
  being held.

* relay: the LISTEN wildcard hostname match is rewritten as an anchored
  suffix comparison. The previous length/prefix-based check could accept
  non-matching hostnames and crashed on hostnames containing no dot;
  hostnames with an empty left label (".example.com") are now rejected.

* build: bin/snif-relay is now installed by `make install`.

Version 1.04
------------

* build: fix `make dist` so the release tarball is complete. bin/snif-conn
  (declared bin_SCRIPTS, which automake treats as generated) and the
  etc/snif.conf + etc/systemd/snif-conn.service files consumed by
  install-data-hook were missing from the distribution, breaking a build
  from the tarball (they were present in a git checkout). No functional
  change to the connector or relay.

Version 1.03
------------

* snifdog: new CA Transparency watchdog (M1), a standalone sub-package that
  monitors public CT logs for certificates issued under a SNIF namespace and
  validates them against name-bound CSRs, backstopping the relay-cannot-
  impersonate guarantee. Ships its own configure.ac; see snifdog/README.
  Includes a first-run crt.sh history backfill with incremental checkpointing
  and transient-failure backoff.

* docker: official SNIF Connector container image (Dockerfile + docker/); the
  image is published to GHCR automatically on each version tag.

* cert: the connector key algorithm is now configurable, and the CA proxy may
  steer the choice via an X-SNIF-KeyAlgo response header.

* cert: CA proxy issuance errors are delivered to the connector instead of
  hanging on an endless 503.

* ca-proxy: the Turnstile sitekey is server-pinned; added a mkdirs bootstrap
  script.

Version 1.02
------------

* Live in-browser demo (wasm connector + CA-proxy captcha issuance path),
  comparison table.

Version 1.01
------------

* Relicensed to Apache-2.0; added CI, a security policy, and community docs.
